r V4.1, a No on a multi-subject bill can't be tied to either part. The bill-text page is in the same record group and supplies the notification provision.\n- **Her failed Amendment #11 (Roll Call 335, 14–34):** I coded it `author` on the motion page and `vote` on the roll-call page. Schools would still notify parents about any request that stands. A student could withdraw the request to avoid that, and the school then couldn't use the new name. This weakens the notification mandate but doesn't match rung 1 (\"keep … from parents unless the student consents\") or rung 2. I coded it `direction-only`.\n- **Her press statement of 2023-04-10:** coded `statement-other`. She opposes a state mandate and says the districts' own name and pronoun policies \"have worked well\". That leans against rung 4, but it names no rung's clause, so it is `direction-only`.\n\n`rests_on` is empty because the row is blank.\n\n**Judgement call:** the amendment could be read as rung 4, since it still requires notice before staff use the new name. I coded it `direction-only` instead, so the coders may disagree on this row.\n\n**Quotes:**\n- The district-policies sentence is tier `direction` and quotable.\n- The \"losing homes and support\" sentence is tier `none` and not quotable (`not-forward`).\n\n**`needs_source`:**\n- Her floor explanation of Amendment #11 in the Senate Journal of April 6, 2023.\n- Any first-party answer from her on whether schools should tell parents.","ttft_ms":34006,"type":"result","duration_ms":59736,"uuid":"9fcb96bb-92c1-4568-9936-39b3399b47ad","ttft_stream_ms":1078,"time_to_request_ms":70,"first_content_frame_ms":1078,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
